course: dictionary
lesson: 7453
title: One word, many meanings
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Many words have more than one meaning, and the context tells you which one the writer means. «Poop» can mean solid waste that comes out of a body, or it can be a word for feeling very tired.
explain: Look at the words around it. In «...much of the sand under your feet may have passed through a parrotfish, which crunches coral with its beak and poops out fine sand», poops is a verb: it means to pass solid waste. In «I'm pooped!» the word means worn out, so it is about feeling tired, not about a body.
explain: «Poop» is informal and a bit childish, so save it for chatting with friends. In a school report or a letter to a grown-up, use poo, or droppings for animals.
fact: Poop can be a noun, a verb, or an adjective, depending on its form: «pooped» is the adjective.
fact: «Pooped» means very tired, as in «I'm pooped after sport!»
fact: «Poop-poop» is the sound of an old motor-car's horn, squeezed from a rubber bulb.
fact: The poop deck is the raised deck at the back of an old ship.
fact: Animal poop, called droppings or dung, can tell scientists what an animal has been eating.
exercise: «...which crunches coral with its beak and poops out fine sand.» What does poops mean here? | To crunch and eat coral | To jump into the sea | *To pass solid waste | To glow in the dark
exercise: «I'm pooped after the football match!» What does pooped mean? | Very happy | Very hungry | *Very tired | Very wet
exercise: Which phrase names the raised deck at the back of an old ship? | Party pooper | Poop scoop | Dog poop | *Poop deck
test: «...at intervals he faintly murmured "Poop-poop!"» What is the "Poop-poop" sound in this story? | *The horn of a motor-car | A kind of sandwich | The name of a river | Someone sneezing
test: Which word is the most polite choice in a school report about animal waste? | Poop-poop | Party pooper | Pooped | *Droppings
exam: Which sentence uses poop as a noun? | *Please pick up the dog poop before you wash your hands. | The parrotfish poops out fine sand. | I'm pooped after the race. | The dog pooped on the lawn.
